  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe center of the Nor'easter has just moved on shore. This has \npushed the main rain band northwest over the Mohawk Valley, Finger \nLakes Region, and central portions of the Southern Tier. Elsewhere, \nsteady rain has ended though drizzle and fog have taken its place. \nMRMS shows some additional scattered showers over downstate NY and \nNJ with an area of widespread rain about to push onshore. With the \nlow not moving much anytime soon, rain will continue this afternoon \nand through the overnight hours. The low begins to weaken later \ntoday resulting in the breezy winds gradually becoming calmer \ntonight. Some clearing in sky cover across north-central NY may lead \nto fog development. On Monday, the low will finally slide up the \ncoast as it continues to weaken. That will keep the highest rain \nchances along and east of I-81. Additional rainfall between now and \nearly Monday evening will be less than a half inch across the region.\n\nHigh pressure will slowly build into the region behind the departing \nsystem Monday night, though there may be some stray showers as weak \nwaves try to take advantage of wraparound moisture. Otherwise, winds \nwill become calm and model soundings show low-level moisture in \naddition to any moisture added from recent rainfall. This will \nlikely lead to widespread fog and/or low stratus Monday night into \nTuesday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will continue to build into the region on Tuesday. \nThis will bring a stretch of quieter weather conditions through \nmidweek. Southwest to westerly flow will advect in much warmer air \nand push daytime highs into the 70s. Valley locations may even reach \n80 on Thursday.\n\nRain will return late in the week though there remains uncertainty \nwith timing. A broad low pressure system will set up over Canada. A \nfrontal system will extend from the low all the way back into the \nMidwest. This frontal system will push in as early as Thursday night \nand move through on Friday. While there are some timing differences, \nmodel guidance favor high pressure building in early Saturday behind \nthis front's departure which would favor a dry yet cooler start to \nthe weekend. High pressure will stick around into Sunday though just \nhow long it can delay the arrival of the next system remains \nuncertain.",
